# Ephemeral / machine-local Claude runtime state that lands here when this dir is
# the CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR of the worktree (clean-split model: config-home = .claude,
# not .claude/hub). Per-worktree, per-machine — never committed.
.claude.json
projects/
sessions/
session-env/
shell-snapshots/
history.jsonl
file-history/
cache/
paste-cache/
backups/
ide/
plugins/
mcp-needs-auth-cache.json
.last-cleanup
plans/
telemetry/

# Session checkpoints — private, auto-generated before compaction.
# ANCHORED with a leading slash: unanchored, it also matched
# `.claude/memory/checkpoint-identity-to-seam-backlog.md`, a real memory file whose
# name merely starts with the same word — so that file could never be committed, and
# MEMORY.md's "Known debt" recorded the link it fixes as dead while the file sat
# untracked on one machine. A name-shaped rule must say WHERE it applies.
/checkpoint-*.md

# Diagram preview scratch — overwritten every query, never committed
claude-preview*.adoc
# Design brainstorm scratch — working notes for a session, never committed
claude-brainstorm-*.adoc
# Any whiteboard, whatever it is named. The two patterns above only caught the names WE
# happened to use, so a session's whiteboard under any other name leaked into commits by
# default — `opencode-control-plane-whiteboard.adoc` (52 KB of another session's live
# notes) was swept in that way on 2026-09-29. A whiteboard declares itself in its first
# line; the filename convention is what git can act on, so enforce the suffix.
*-whiteboard.adoc
diag-*.svg

# nixos-node-substrate migration working notes — living scratch, never committed
nixos-*-progress.md

# Handoffs are session-scoped, never committed. REVERSES the earlier policy, which said the
# finalized `*-handoff.md` notes at this level WERE tracked. Nothing here is secret — the
# reason is lifecycle: a handoff exists to pass work to the next session, and it is superseded
# the moment that work lands. Tracking them accretes stale intent in the tree, which is exactly
# what the preview and whiteboard rules above avoid. Same family as `nixos-*-progress.md`:
# living scratch, not a deliverable. What survives a handoff belongs in `docs/` or in memory.
# Three were already tracked when the rule flipped on 2026-10-02
# (`cellar-secrets-design-handoff.md`, `doctor-frontier-handoff-note.md`,
# `worktree-foundation-handoff.md`) and were untracked in the same change.
# ANCHORED: a bare pattern would also match `.claude/hub/` and `.claude/memory/` — the same
# unanchored-pattern trap as `checkpoint-*.md` and `memory/` elsewhere in this file.
# Both spellings exist in the wild: `-handoff.md` and `-handoff-note.md`.
/*handoff*.md

# Machine-local policy-limits snapshot — runtime state, never committed
policy-limits.json

# Per-conversation git worktrees (Workspace isolation rule) — never committed
worktrees/

# Local, machine-specific Claude settings
settings.local.json

# The knowledge base is NOT here — it lives on the orphan `memory` branch, checked out at
# `<repo>.d/memory`, and sessions point `autoMemoryDirectory` at that worktree. Ignored so
# a stray write into a CODE worktree can never be committed and re-fork the tree (two
# divergent trees once made a dead-link audit record 7 live facts as dead).
# ANCHORED: bare `memory/` would also match `.claude/hub/memory/`, the hub subtree's own
# memory, which MUST stay tracked — the same unanchored-pattern trap as `checkpoint-*.md`
# above.
/memory/

# Track hook scripts: counter the root .gitignore's generic [Bb]in/ rule
!bin/
!bin/*.sh

# Claude's auto-memory. The knowledge base is NOT here — it lives on the orphan `memory`
# branch, checked out at `<repo>.d/memory`, and sessions point `autoMemoryDirectory` there.
# ANCHORED: a bare `memory/` would also match `.claude/hub/memory/`, the hub subtree's own
# memory, which MUST stay tracked.
/memory/

# Backups written by the Write|Edit PreToolUse hook (bin/../hooks/file-backup-hook.js).
# They are copies of files already under version control, so committing them stores the
# same content twice and leaks work-in-progress into review.
# ★ The hook writes to `<cwd>/.claude/.edit-baks/`, so its location follows the session's
# working directory — a session whose cwd drifted to another worktree drops backups there.
# Measured 2026-10-03: one such file was COMMITTED on `feature/ssot-manifest` (140 lines of
# flake.nix), and two landed in the orphan `memory` checkout.
# ANCHORED: a bare `.edit-baks/` would also match inside `.claude/hub/`, the subtree we do
# not own — same trap as `/memory/` above.
/.edit-baks/

# Session tags read by the seat-roster extension. Keyed by `sessionId`, which is
# per-seat and per-process: a tag file names a session that exists on THIS machine
# and nowhere else, so committing them would publish one seat's live state.
# ★ Third instance of the same trap in one day — the other two were 13 permission
# entries that landed in the TRACKED settings.json, and the edit-hook backups.
/seat-roster/
